  "summary": "When the server answers with an error status, a client call fails with the error code `HTTP_STATUS`. Every Tac service language reads the status number, such as 422, and, when the answer's media type is `application/json` or `application/problem+json`, reads the JSON body into a model with the same reader it uses for a success answer. Here the server checks the order with `Check` and answers 422 with a problem body (RFC 9457): its `title`, its `status` and one entry per broken rule in `errors`. The page shows the status, the title and how many rules the order broke. Go reads the body with `BehaviorErrorBody(err, &problem)` and the status with `BehaviorErrorStatus(err)`; Rust with `error.body_as::<Problem>()` and `error.status`; C# with `error.TryReadBody(out Problem problem)` and `error.Status`; F# with `TacMappingModels.errorBodyProblem error`; Swift with `Behaviors.errorBody(error, as: Problem.self)`; Kotlin with `Behaviors.errorBody<Problem>(error)`; Java with `Behaviors.errorBody(error, TacModels::readProblem)`; Dart with `BehaviorErrors.body(error, tachyonReadModelProblem)`; Ruby, Python and Lua with `BehaviorError.body_of(error, Problem)`; C++ with `error.readBodyFromJson<Problem>()`; JavaScript and TypeScript with `Behaviors.errorBody(error)` and `Behaviors.errorStatus(error)`. Each reader answers \"no body\" (`false`, `null`, `None`, `nil` or an empty optional) when the error has no body or the body has another shape. JavaScript, TypeScript and Lua do not check the body's field types. The transport leaves the body out when the answer has another media type, is not valid JSON, is `null`, holds a number that is not safe in JavaScript, or is larger than the request's answer limit (1 MiB at most): the error then has the status only. A test fake answers the same way: a TestSetup adapter returns or throws the language's error with `HTTP_STATUS`, a status from 400 to 599 and a body, for example `&BehaviorError{Code: \"HTTP_STATUS\", Status: 409, Body: body}` in Go or `Behaviors.httpError(409, body)` in JavaScript. The call gets the status and the body, never the adapter's own message. Native Rust and native C# services keep the status only (ADR 0117). The server half of this example is a plain JavaScript controller. A controller in TypeScript, Python, PHP, Java, Groovy, C#, F#, Kotlin, Rust, Go, Dart, Ruby, Lua or C++ answers a failed `Check` in the same way (see Validation), so this section shows only the browser side in other languages.",
